WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:08.170
These images have shocked the world: Venezuelan
soldiers preventing much needed aid from coming

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:11.710
into the country from neighboring Columbia.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:16.630
But while the aid is kept OUT, a steady stream
of Venezuelans pour into the Columbian city

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:21.670
of Cucuta over the nearby Simon Bolivar Bridge.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:27.470
Leaving behind hyperinflation and empty supermarket
shelves, these people exit via an official

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.480
border crossing.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.230
Many others have not been able to obtain a
passport, and are forced to leave the country

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.179
via smuggling routes cutting through the dry
riverbed.

00:00:38.179 --> 00:00:43.300
Known as "trochas," they are controlled by
criminal gangs, who extract a heavy transit

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.870
fee from the migrants.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:48.370
It's a price they are willing to pay in the
hope of finding of work and a better life

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:49.679
in Colombia.

00:00:49.679 --> 00:00:58.109
"I'm going to Medellin because I have family
there and we will work, because there is nothing

00:00:58.109 --> 00:00:59.109
in Venezuela.

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:03.760
The more you wait, the worse the situation
in Venezuela will get."

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.970
Since neighboring countries demand that Venezuelans
show a passport, more and more people are

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.330
using the trochas to leave.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:16.610
With no money for any transport, they often
have to walk for hundreds of miles.

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.370
It's a business opportunity for shoemaker
Domingo Montilla, who also fled across the

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:23.580
border.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:37.870
"The shoes from the people that cross often
are damaged, they are falling apart.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:44.430
So I sew them, for anybody who needs it."

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:50.010
Even regular commuters have taken to the trochas,
which snake through the riverbed only meters

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.480
from the bridge.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.490
Carlos Carretero carries goods across the
border to sell in Venezuela on the black market.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:03.380
Customs officers have clamped down on the
cross border trade, forcing Carlos off road

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.770
to continue his work.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:16.160
"If migration doesn't let us work, we don’t
have any other choice but to use the trochas."

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.780
Carlos earns about 4 euros a day shuttling
goods across the border - barely enough to

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:21.780
get by.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.120
While for many the trochas are an escape route,
for him they are a lifeline.

